      { q: "Who issues deck permits in Great Falls VA?", a: "Great Falls is within unincorporated Fairfax County. Deck permits are issued by the Fairfax County Department of Land Development Services. Permits are required for decks 30 inches or more above grade or greater than 200 square feet. Large or complex Great Falls projects — particularly those with tall post systems or significant elevation changes — may require stamped structural drawings from a licensed PE." },
      { q: "Are there stream buffer restrictions for decks in Great Falls VA?", a: "Yes, for properties near waterways. Fairfax County's Resource Protection Area (RPA) regulations restrict construction within 100 feet of stream banks — including Difficult Run, Colvin Run, and their tributaries, which run through many Great Falls properties. If your lot is near a stream or stream buffer, the deck placement must account for the RPA setback. LDN Decks reviews RPA applicability at the outset of every Great Falls project." },
      { q: "How much does a deck cost in Great Falls VA?", a: "Great Falls deck projects exceed Northern Virginia medians due to scope and specification. A 400-square-foot composite deck runs $45,000 to $70,000. Multi-level composite decks at 600 square feet run $75,000 to $125,000. Premium AZEK or hardwood decks at 400 square feet run $60,000 to $95,000. Large integrated outdoor living projects (deck, pergola, outdoor kitchen) run $100,000 to $200,000+." },
      { q: "Can I build a very large deck in Great Falls VA?", a: "Great Falls' large-lot zoning accommodates large deck footprints — lot coverage limits on one-to-five-acre parcels are rarely a constraint for deck additions. Setback requirements (typically 30 to 50 feet from rear and side lines in low-density zones) must be respected, and properties near stream buffers have additional constraints. Subject to these limits, Great Falls' design freedom and lot size support the largest residential deck projects in Northern Virginia." }
