    category: 'Local Service Areas',
    tags: ['deck builder alexandria va', 'deck contractor alexandria virginia', 'alexandria deck building', 'composite deck alexandria va', 'city of alexandria deck permit', 'old town alexandria deck', 'deck company alexandria va 2026'],
    sourceLinks: COMPOSITE_BRAND_SOURCE_LINKS,
    excerpt: "Alexandria is an independent city with some of the most distinctive permitting and design review requirements in Northern Virginia. Old Town Alexandria's historic district is the most protected historic overlay in the region, with Architectural Review Board oversight that applies rigorous standards to any exterior modification including decks. Outside the historic core, Alexandria's neighborhoods range from mid-century suburban to newer mixed-use development.",
    content: "Alexandria's deck market is shaped by two realities that operate simultaneously: a historic core with some of the strictest design review standards in Virginia, and a surrounding city of established neighborhoods that permit more straightforwardly through the standard city process. Understanding which applies to your specific property is the starting point for any Alexandria deck project.\n\n## City of Alexandria Permit Authority\n\nAlexandria is an independent incorporated city. All deck permits within city limits are issued by the City of Alexandria Department of Planning and Zoning. Alexandria is not part of Fairfax County — the two are adjacent but entirely separate jurisdictions. There is no ambiguity here: if your property is within the City of Alexandria, you permit through the city.\n\nCity of Alexandria deck permit requirements:\n- Building permit required for decks 30 inches or more above grade or greater than 200 square feet\n- Site plan showing lot, existing structures, proposed deck, and setbacks\n- Structural drawings: footing (21-inch frost depth), framing plan, ledger detail, railing specifications\n- Zoning compliance: City of Alexandria zoning setback and lot coverage requirements — these are often more restrictive in older city neighborhoods than suburban county standards\n\n## Old Town Alexandria Historic District\n\nOld Town Alexandria contains one of the most protected historic districts in Virginia. The Architectural Review Board (ARB) has jurisdiction over exterior modifications to properties within the Old Town Historic District. For deck projects on historic properties, this means:\n\n**ARB approval is required** before a building permit can be issued for any exterior modification visible from a public street or alley.\n\n**ARB standards for decks in Old Town:**\n- Decks must be located at the rear of the property (not visible from the street front)\n- Materials must be compatible with the historic character — composite decking in natural wood-tone colors is generally more favorably reviewed than bright or non-traditional colors\n- Deck height, railing design, and any lighting or screening elements are subject to ARB review\n- Decks that are not visible from any public right-of-way have a simpler review path than those with any street or alley visibility\n\n**ARB review timeline:** A full ARB hearing is required for applications that don't qualify for staff-level approval. ARB hearings are scheduled monthly — depending on when your application is submitted, the wait for a hearing slot can add 4 to 8 weeks beyond the permit review timeline.\n\nFor Old Town properties, plan for a total timeline of 10 to 18 weeks from design to permit, not including construction.\n\n## Del Ray, Rosemont, Seminary Hill, and Other Alexandria Neighborhoods\n\nOutside the Old Town Historic District, Alexandria's neighborhoods permit without ARB review, though the city's zoning requirements remain. Key characteristics:\n\n**Del Ray:** Established neighborhood north of Old Town with active civic associations. Del Ray Civic Association does not have formal design review authority, but community character considerations influence material and color choices.\n\n**Rosemont:** Older established neighborhood. Mostly non-HOA single-family. Standard city permit process.\n\n**Seminary Hill and Mark Center area:** Mix of single-family and townhouse, mostly non-HOA. Standard city process.\n\n**Newer Alexandria developments (Carlyle, Potomac Yard, Eisenhower):** Condo and mixed-use areas where deck additions typically don't apply. Single-family and townhouse product in these newer developments often have community HOAs with design review.\n\n## Alexandria Deck Costs 2026\n\n- **Composite deck replacement (300 sq ft, non-historic neighborhoods):** $28,000 to $45,000\n- **New composite deck addition (300 sq ft):** $30,000 to $48,000\n- **Old Town historic property deck (including ARB process and materials compliance):** $34,000 to $55,000\n- **Townhouse deck addition (100-150 sq ft):** $11,000 to $20,000\n- **Multi-level deck (400-500 sq ft):** $52,000 to $82,000\n\nOld Town projects carry premium cost due to the ARB process timeline and materials compliance requirements.\n\n[Contact LDN Decks for an Alexandria VA Deck Estimate](/get-estimate)\n\n---\n\n**Related:** [Deck Builder Lorton VA](/blog/deck-builder-lorton-va) · [Deck Builder Springfield VA](/blog/deck-builder-springfield-va) · [HOA Deck Approval Process](/blog/hoa-deck-approval-process-northern-virginia) · [Deck Cost Northern Virginia 2026](/blog/how-much-does-deck-cost-northern-virginia-2026) · [New Deck Services](/services/new-decks)",
    faq: [
      { q: "Who issues deck permits in Alexandria VA?", a: "Alexandria is an independent city. All deck permits are issued by the City of Alexandria Department of Planning and Zoning. Alexandria is entirely separate from Fairfax County — there is no jurisdictional ambiguity. Permits are required for decks 30 inches or more above grade or greater than 200 square feet. Properties in Old Town Alexandria's Historic District require Architectural Review Board approval before a building permit is issued." },
      { q: "Does Old Town Alexandria require historic review for deck permits?", a: "Yes. Old Town Alexandria's Historic District is governed by the City of Alexandria Architectural Review Board (ARB). Any exterior modification to a property within the historic district — including deck additions — requires ARB approval before a building permit is issued. ARB hearings are monthly; the process adds 4 to 8 weeks beyond standard permit review. Total timeline from design to permit on Old Town historic properties is typically 10 to 18 weeks." },
      { q: "What materials does Alexandria's ARB accept for decks?", a: "For Old Town Historic District properties, the ARB requires materials compatible with the historic character of the property. Composite decking in natural wood-tone colors is generally more favorably reviewed than bright or non-traditional colors. Decks must be located at the rear of the property (not visible from the street front). Railing design, height, and any lighting are also subject to ARB review. Rear decks with no visibility from any public right-of-way have a simpler review path." },
      { q: "How much does a deck cost in Alexandria VA?", a: "Non-historic Alexandria neighborhoods: composite replacement runs $28,000 to $45,000 for 300 sq ft; new additions run $30,000 to $48,000. Old Town historic properties run $34,000 to $55,000 due to the ARB process timeline and materials compliance requirements. Townhouse additions run $11,000 to $20,000. Multi-level decks run $52,000 to $82,000." },
      { q: "Is Alexandria the same jurisdiction as Fairfax County for deck permits?", a: "No. The City of Alexandria is an independent incorporated city, entirely separate from Fairfax County. They are adjacent jurisdictions but have separate city and county governments, separate permitting departments, separate zoning ordinances, and separate fee schedules. There is no overlap — your property is either in Alexandria or in Fairfax County, never both. Always confirm your parcel's jurisdiction before submitting any permit application." }
    ],
