      { q: "Does Laurel Hill HOA approve composite decking?", a: "Yes. The Laurel Hill Community Association accepts composite decking from major brands in color families consistent with the home's exterior and community character. Earth tones and neutral palettes are the most consistently approved. Submissions require scaled drawings, material specifications, and color references. LHCA review typically takes 30 to 45 days for complete submissions. LDN Decks prepares LHCA submissions as part of every Laurel Hill project." },
      { q: "How much does a deck cost in Lorton VA?", a: "Composite deck replacement in Laurel Hill or a newer Lorton home runs $28,000 to $44,000 for 300 square feet. New composite additions in Laurel Hill run $30,000 to $47,000. Older Lorton neighborhood replacements, which commonly include structural remediation work, run $30,000 to $50,000. Multi-level decks run $52,000 to $82,000. Townhouse deck additions run $11,000 to $20,000." },
      { q: "What is the Laurel Hill community in Lorton?", a: "Laurel Hill is one of the largest planned communities in southern Fairfax County, built from the early 2000s through the 2010s on the former grounds of the Lorton Reformatory federal prison complex. It spans several thousand homes across multiple development phases and is governed by the Laurel Hill Community Association. All exterior modifications require LHCA design review approval before Fairfax County permits are applied for." },
      { q: "Are there deed restrictions near Fort Belvoir in Lorton?", a: "Some Lorton-area properties near the Fort Belvoir perimeter have deed restrictions or covenants from the period of the base's operational expansion. These are separate from HOA governance and must be verified at the deed level for any property near Fort Belvoir. LDN Decks checks for relevant deed covenants during project assessment for properties near the base area, as these can affect where on the lot a deck can be constructed." }
