    sourceLinks: COMPOSITE_BRAND_SOURCE_LINKS,
    excerpt: "Oakton is an unincorporated Fairfax County community along the Route 123 corridor between Vienna and Fairfax. It is one of the county's premium residential areas — characterized by larger lot sizes than most of Fairfax County, a housing stock that skews toward 1970s through 1990s custom and semi-custom construction, and some of the highest average home values in the county outside of McLean and Great Falls.",
    content: "Oakton occupies a distinctive position in the Fairfax County market: a premium residential area with larger lots, mature landscaping, and a housing stock that was built to higher specifications than the county's typical suburban production. For deck work, this translates to above-average project scopes, higher composite material adoption, and — because the housing stock is 30 to 50 years old — a significant replacement market.\n\n## Permit Authority: Fairfax County\n\nOakton is in unincorporated Fairfax County. All deck permits are issued by the Fairfax County Department of Land Development Services:\n\n- Building permit required for decks 30 inches or more above grade or greater than 200 square feet\n- Site plan showing lot, existing structures, proposed deck, and all setbacks\n- Structural drawings: footing (21-inch frost depth), framing plan, ledger attachment, railing specifications\n- Plan review: 4 to 8 weeks for standard residential decks\n\n**Larger lot sizes and setback opportunity:** Oakton's larger lots — many in the half-acre to two-acre range — provide more setback clearance than the county average, allowing for larger deck footprints and more flexible placement without running into coverage or setback constraints.\n\n## Oakton's HOA Landscape\n\nOakton's HOA landscape is more varied than highly planned communities like Burke Centre or Laurel Hill:\n\n**Established single-family neighborhoods (no HOA):** Many of Oakton's original single-family subdivisions from the 1970s and 1980s were developed without HOA governance. The Waples Mill area, parts of Hunter Mill Road corridor, and many of the larger-lot subdivisions have no active HOA. In these neighborhoods, Fairfax County permit compliance is the primary constraint.\n\n**Planned communities and newer developments:** Some Oakton-area planned communities from the 1980s and 1990s have active HOAs with exterior modification review. These are the minority in Oakton but are present in certain corridors.\n\n**Oakton's townhouse communities:** Active HOAs in most cases. Townhouse deck additions require association approval.\n\nVerify your specific neighborhood's HOA status — the range across Oakton is significant and cannot be assumed from the address alone.\n\n## The Oakton Replacement Market\n\nOakton's housing stock — concentrated in the 1975 to 1995 construction era — is at the center of the premium replacement market cycle. Custom and semi-custom homes built in this era were frequently built with large original wood decks (500 to 800 square feet or more) as a significant architectural feature. These decks are now 30 to 50 years old and showing the full range of structural issues:\n\n**Large original deck structures:** Oakton's custom homes often have substantial original deck configurations — multi-level, with integrated stairs, potentially with structural pergola or shade elements. Replacement projects on these homes are typically larger and more complex than the county average.\n\n**Premium material upgrades:** Oakton homeowners replacing aging wood decks almost universally select premium composite product lines — Trex Transcend, TimberTech Vintage Reserve, AZEK Harvest Collection. The original deck quality drives expectations for the replacement.\n\n**Integrated landscape features:** Many Oakton properties have mature landscaping integrated with the existing deck — hardscaping, grade changes, planting beds adjacent to or beneath the deck. Replacement projects must account for how the new deck interacts with the existing landscape.\n\n## Oakton Deck Costs 2026\n\n- **Composite deck replacement (300 sq ft):** $31,000 to $50,000\n- **Large composite deck replacement (500-800 sq ft, premium spec):** $65,000 to $120,000\n- **New composite deck addition (300 sq ft):** $33,000 to $53,000\n- **Multi-level deck with integrated stairs (400-500 sq ft):** $60,000 to $95,000\n\nOakton's project ranges are among the highest in the LDN Decks service area — both because of larger average footprints and because homeowners consistently select premium material and feature specifications.\n\n[Contact LDN Decks for an Oakton VA Deck Estimate](/get-estimate)\n\n---\n\n**Related:** [Deck Builder Vienna VA](/blog/deck-builder-vienna-va) · [Deck Builder McLean VA](/blog/deck-builder-mclean-va) · [Best Composite Deck Colors Full Sun Northern Virginia](/blog/best-composite-deck-colors-full-sun-northern-virginia) · [How Long Does a Composite Deck Last](/blog/how-long-does-composite-deck-last-northern-virginia) · [New Deck Services](/services/new-decks)",
