    sourceLinks: COMPOSITE_BRAND_SOURCE_LINKS,
    excerpt: "South Riding is one of Loudoun County's largest and most established planned communities — a master-planned development of approximately 6,500 homes built from the mid-1990s through the mid-2000s in the eastern Loudoun corridor between Chantilly and Dulles. Its size, age, and highly active community association make it one of the most structured HOA environments for deck work in the county.",
    content: "South Riding is a benchmark planned community in eastern Loudoun County — large enough that it functions as a small city in practice, with its own retail, schools, and community facilities, governed by the South Riding Proprietary, one of the most organized and detailed community associations in the region. For deck work, South Riding's combination of HOA governance requirements, a housing stock that is now 20 to 30 years old, and Loudoun County permit requirements creates a well-defined process that LDN Decks has navigated many times.\n\n## Permit Authority: Loudoun County\n\nSouth Riding is in unincorporated Loudoun County. Deck permits are issued by the Loudoun County Department of Building and Development:\n\n- Building permit required for decks 30 inches or more above grade or greater than 200 square feet\n- Site plan showing lot, existing structures, proposed deck, and all setbacks\n- Structural drawings: footing (21-inch frost depth), framing plan, ledger attachment detail, railing specifications\n- Plan review: 4 to 8 weeks for standard residential decks\n- South Riding Proprietary approval required before county permit application is submitted\n\n## South Riding Proprietary Design Review\n\nThe South Riding Proprietary is the master community association governing the entire South Riding development. All exterior modifications — including deck additions and replacements — require Proprietary review and approval before a Loudoun County permit can be applied for.\n\n**South Riding Proprietary review standards for decks:**\n- Deck footprint and relationship to the lot and adjacent properties\n- Decking material: composite from major brands (Trex, TimberTech, AZEK) is accepted; natural wood-tone color families are the most consistently approved\n- Railing style and color: must be compatible with the home's architecture and the community's character\n- Fencing, screening, or privacy elements integrated with the deck require separate disclosure in the application\n- Submission requirements: complete ARC application including site plan, elevation drawings, material specifications with manufacturer color references, and photographs of the existing home\n- Review timeline: 30 days for standard submissions; incomplete submissions are returned\n\n**Village associations:** South Riding is divided into villages, some of which have their own sub-association in addition to the master Proprietary. In some cases, village-level approval is required in addition to Proprietary approval. LDN Decks confirms the applicable approval chain at project outset for every South Riding project.\n\n## The South Riding Replacement Market\n\nSouth Riding's housing stock (1995-2005 construction era) is entering the core of its deck replacement cycle. Original pressure-treated wood decks from this era are now 20 to 30 years old — the age at which surface deterioration is visible and structural issues are beginning to emerge. This is the sweet spot of the replacement market: old enough to need replacement, new enough that the house structure itself is in good condition.\n\nReplacement projects in South Riding are typically clean composite upgrades — the original deck structures were built to code, ledger connections are generally sound, and the replacement scope is primarily surface and railing rather than structural remediation. Homeowners upgrading at this age typically select mid-to-upper composite product lines (Trex Select or Trex Enhance Naturals, TimberTech Pro or TimberTech Legacy) with railing upgrades to aluminum or composite railing systems.\n\n## South Riding Deck Costs 2026\n\n- **Composite deck replacement (300 sq ft):** $27,000 to $43,000\n- **New composite deck addition (300 sq ft):** $29,000 to $46,000\n- **Townhouse deck addition (100-150 sq ft):** $11,000 to $20,000\n- **Multi-level deck (400-500 sq ft):** $50,000 to $80,000\n\n[Contact LDN Decks for a South Riding VA Deck Estimate](/get-estimate)\n\n---\n\n**Related:** [Deck Builder Ashburn VA](/blog/deck-builder-ashburn-va) · [Deck Builder Sterling VA](/blog/deck-builder-sterling-va) · [HOA Deck Approval Process](/blog/hoa-deck-approval-process-northern-virginia) · [Composite vs Pressure-Treated Deck Total Cost](/blog/composite-vs-pressure-treated-deck-total-cost-20-years) · [New Deck Services](/services/new-decks)",
