    content: "Aldie's deck market is almost entirely defined by its planned communities. The historic Aldie village itself is tiny — a crossroads community on Route 50 with a mill and a handful of historic properties. But the surrounding area has been transformed by large-scale planned community development, making Aldie's zip code home to thousands of homes in HOA-governed environments with active design review.\n\n## Permit Authority: Loudoun County\n\nAll Aldie-area properties are in unincorporated Loudoun County. Deck permits are issued by the Loudoun County Department of Building and Development:\n\n- Building permit required for decks 30 inches or more above grade or greater than 200 square feet\n- Site plan showing lot, existing structures, proposed deck, and all setbacks\n- Structural drawings: footing (21-inch frost depth), framing plan, ledger attachment, railing specifications\n- Plan review: 4 to 8 weeks for standard residential decks\n- HOA approval must be obtained before county permit application is submitted\n\n## Stone Ridge Community\n\nStone Ridge is one of the largest planned communities in central Loudoun County, located along the Route 50 corridor east of Aldie. The Stone Ridge Community Association governs exterior modifications:\n\n- Composite decking from major brands in approved natural color families is accepted\n- ARC submission required: site plan, elevation drawings, material specifications, color references, photographs\n- Review timeline: 30 to 45 days for complete submissions\n- Stone Ridge is divided into sections with slightly varying design standards — LDN Decks confirms the applicable section standards at project outset\n\n## Willowsford\n\nWillowsford is one of the most distinctive planned communities in Northern Virginia — a farm-themed community centered on the Willowsford Farm and Grange, built from the early 2010s with a strong emphasis on community agricultural and natural features. The Willowsford Community Association has detailed architectural standards that reflect the community's character:\n\n- Deck materials must be consistent with Willowsford's architectural aesthetic — natural material appearances are strongly preferred\n- Composite decking in natural wood-tone finishes is the primary accepted product; bright or non-natural colors are not typically approved\n- Railing design must be compatible with the home's architectural style, which skews toward craftsman and farmhouse-inspired designs in Willowsford\n- ARC submission: complete package with drawings, materials, and color references\n- Review timeline: 30 to 45 days\n\nWillowsford's community character is one of the most distinctive in Loudoun County and influences material and design decisions more than most HOAs.\n\n## Other Aldie-Area Communities\n\n**Virginia Oaks and Kirkpatrick Farms:** Established communities in the Aldie/Chantilly border area with active HOAs.\n\n**Bull Run Mountain Estates and rural Aldie properties:** West of the planned community corridor, properties become more rural with larger lots and no HOA governance.\n\n## Aldie Area Deck Costs 2026\n\n- **Composite deck replacement (300 sq ft, Stone Ridge or standard community):** $28,000 to $44,000\n- **Composite deck replacement (300 sq ft, Willowsford premium spec):** $31,000 to $50,000\n- **New composite deck addition (300 sq ft):** $29,000 to $47,000\n- **Multi-level deck (400-500 sq ft):** $52,000 to $83,000\n\nWillowsford projects carry premium ranges reflecting the community's emphasis on higher-quality natural-appearance products.\n\n[Contact LDN Decks for an Aldie VA Deck Estimate](/get-estimate)\n\n---\n\n**Related:** [Deck Builder Broadlands VA](/blog/deck-builder-broadlands-va) · [Deck Builder Ashburn VA](/blog/deck-builder-ashburn-va) · [HOA Deck Approval Process](/blog/hoa-deck-approval-process-northern-virginia) · [Best Composite Deck Colors Full Sun Northern Virginia](/blog/best-composite-deck-colors-full-sun-northern-virginia) · [New Deck Services](/services/new-decks)",
