    excerpt: "Haymarket is a town in western Prince William County along Route 15 at Interstate 66. The Haymarket corridor has seen significant residential development in the 2000s through 2020s, anchored by Dominion Valley Country Club and other planned communities — most Haymarket deck projects involve newer HOA-governed homes in an active new addition and upgrade market.",
    content: "Haymarket is a small town in Prince William County in western Prince William, situated along Route 15 at the interchange with Interstate 66. The Haymarket corridor has seen significant residential development from the 2000s onward, particularly through planned communities like Dominion Valley Country Club. Most Haymarket deck projects involve newer homes in HOA-governed communities, with a growing market for new deck additions and early composite upgrades.\n\n## Permit Authority: Prince William County\n\nHaymarket is a town in Prince William County. Building permits are issued by the Prince William County Department of Development Services:\n\n- Building permit required for attached decks and for detached decks over 200 square feet or 30 inches above grade\n- Site plan, structural drawings: footing plan (21-inch frost depth), framing plan, ledger attachment, railing specifications\n- Plan review: 10 to 20 business days for standard residential deck permits\n- HOA approval required before permit application for properties in governed communities\n\n## Dominion Valley Country Club\n\nDominion Valley Country Club is the largest and most prominent planned community in the Haymarket corridor. Developed from the early 2000s onward, Dominion Valley is a golf community with a comprehensive HOA and an active Architectural Review Committee:\n\n**DVCC ARC standards for decks:**\n- Composite decking is strongly favored and is effectively the standard for new and replacement deck work in the community — the premium character of Dominion Valley drives toward higher composite product selections\n- Approved color families align with the community's design standards — natural wood tones and muted contemporary tones within approved manufacturer palettes are consistently approved\n- Railing: aluminum or composite railing in ARC-approved finishes; cable railing is accepted for architecturally compatible homes\n- ARC submission: complete package required — site plan, elevation drawings, material specifications with manufacturer color chips, photographs of existing conditions\n- Review timeline: 30 days for complete submissions\n\nDominion Valley's 2002-2020s housing stock means most homes are within their original deck service life, but the community's growth and premium market position drive active new addition projects and early upgrade work on homes built without a deck or with builder-grade finishes.\n\n## Other Haymarket Communities\n\nThe Route 15 and Route 55 corridors around Haymarket include additional planned communities and subdivisions with varying HOA governance. Each has distinct architectural standards. LDN Decks verifies HOA requirements and specific design standards for each Haymarket property at estimate time before beginning any submission or permit process.\n\n## Haymarket VA Deck Costs 2026\n\n- **Composite deck replacement (300 sq ft, Dominion Valley):** $28,000 to $45,000\n- **New composite deck addition (300 sq ft):** $30,000 to $47,000\n- **Premium composite deck with cable railing (300 sq ft):** $36,000 to $55,000\n- **Multi-level deck (400-500 sq ft, golf course backing):** $55,000 to $90,000\n\n[Contact LDN Decks for a Haymarket VA Deck Estimate](/get-estimate)\n\n---\n\n**Related:** [Deck Builder Manassas VA](/blog/deck-builder-manassas-va) · [Deck Builder Gainesville VA](/blog/deck-builder-gainesville-va) · [HOA Deck Approval Process](/blog/hoa-deck-approval-process-northern-virginia) · [Deck Cost Northern Virginia 2026](/blog/how-much-does-deck-cost-northern-virginia-2026) · [New Deck Services](/services/new-decks)",
